Static ELF review: ELF32, little-endian, RISC-V, entry 0x0 (_start); .highcode_init and .highcode VMA are in linker RAM; load images and .text are in linker Flash; .data/.bss and stack symbol fit the linker-declared RAM range.
Static allocation: two application thread stacks are 1024 bytes each; ISR stack is 2048 bytes; ThreadX timer thread stack is configured as 512 bytes; ELF .data is 8 bytes and .bss is 5564 bytes. Runtime stack watermarks are not measured.
Static review boundary: startup and link symbols, ThreadX context routines, exception stack switch, HPE/PFIC/VTF setup, SysTick setup/clear and ISR call sequence reviewed. Hardware exception semantics, clock frequency, actual tick delivery, thread scheduling, sleep/wakeup and sustained runtime remain unverified.
Release result: build and ELF/link static checks passed; the software release gate is not passed because source tick is 100 ticks/s while the stated requirement is 1000 ticks/s. Verification-hardware design and product schematic/PCB freeze are not released pending user confirmation.
Tick configuration source: TX_TIMER_TICKS_PER_SECOND is 100UL. With source-declared 62,400,000 Hz, SysTick_Config input is 624,000 and core_riscv.h sets CMP to 623,999. This calculation is not a hardware measurement. A stated 1000 ticks/s requirement conflicts with current source and remains unconfirmed.
Simulator check: QEMU, Spike and Renode are not installed in the current host. ThreadX qemu_virt example uses its own QEMU virt startup and linker layout and does not validate the CH585M/WCH path.

